18	
19	### Step wiring
20	
21	Each top-level YAML entry is a developer-chosen step name; the first is the entry
22	point. `class:` names the function. **Give each function class exactly one public
23	method** — several public methods fail at start-up unless every reference adds
24	`method:`.
25	
26	- `next: <step>` — run that step afterwards. This step's return value arrives there
27	  as `@Parameter T`.
28	- `outputs: { <name>: <step> }` — conditional branches. Declare a
29	  `@FunctionalInterface` parameter annotated `@Flow("<name>")` and call it to take the
30	  branch; not calling it short-circuits.
31	
32	### Function parameters
33	
34	Declare only what the step needs; they resolve by role:
35	
36	- `@PathVariable`, `@RequestParam`, `@RequestBody` — Spring MVC annotations work.
37	- Spring beans (repositories, mappers, services) — injected by type as normal.
38	- `ObjectResponse<T>` (`net.officefloor.web.ObjectResponse`) — send the response with
39	  `response.send(dto)`. This is how a step responds; wrap as
40	  `ObjectResponse<ResponseEntity<T>>` to set status explicitly.
41	- `@Parameter T` (`net.officefloor.plugin.section.clazz.Parameter`) — the previous
42	  step's return value, a `@Flow` argument, or a thrown escalation.
43	
44	### Passing state between steps — `Out<T>` / `@Val`
45	
46	Steps do not call each other. Beyond the single `@Parameter` hand-off, publish state
47	into a variable (`net.officefloor.plugin.variable`):
48	
49	- Producer declares `Out<T>` and calls `set(...)`; consumer declares `@Val T`.
50	- Matching is **by type** — two variables of the same type in one pipeline need a
51	  `@Qualifier` annotation to disambiguate.
52	- `@Val` yields the same object the producer stored, **not a copy** — so an `Apply`
53	  step mutates the entity in place and later steps see the change.
54	
55	### Naming
56	
57	Verb plus entity: `Load<E>` (fetch by path variable, publishes `Out<E>`, throws when
58	absent) · `Build<E>` (construct from body) · `Validate<E>` (bind and validate the body,
59	publish it) · `Apply<E>` (mutate) · `Save<E>` · `Delete<E>` · `RespondWith<E>` (200) ·
60	`RespondWith<E>Created` (201) · `RespondWithNoContent` (204).
61	
62	Keep DTOs at the edges — request body in at the first step, response DTO out at the
63	responder. Steps in between work with entities.
64	
65	### Request body and validation
66	
67	- The HTTP body can be read only once: **only one step per pipeline may bind
68	  `@RequestBody`**. A second binding fails at runtime. When later steps need it, the
69	  first step publishes it as a variable.
70	- `@Valid` runs before that step's method body, so step order decides when validation
71	  happens. Put the validating step first — otherwise a missing id returns 404 before an
72	  invalid body can return 400.
73	
74	### Transactions
75	
76	`govern: [ transaction ]` for writes, `govern: [ readonly-transaction ]` for reads,
77	listed on **every** step it covers. Both are provided by the starter. Governance spans
78	the pipeline, so the request commits once at the end.
79	
80	### Errors
81	
82	Functions throw; handlers respond. The exception must be **checked** (`extends
83	Exception`) so it appears in the `throws` clause. Put the handler in
84	`officefloor/escalation/<fully.qualified.ExceptionClass>.yml`, taking the exception as
85	`@Parameter` and responding via `ObjectResponse`. Matching is most-specific-first;
86	anything unmatched falls through to Spring `@RestControllerAdvice`.
87	
88	### Security
89	
90	Guard a whole endpoint file with a Spring Security SpEL expression:
91	
92	```yaml
93	composition:
94	  authorize: "hasRole('OWNER_ADMIN')"
95	```
